  "body": "From the review of PR #990 (#891), non-blocking note: constant surface speed (G96) is clamped by the lower of the machine's `spindle_max` and the tool's `max_rpm`. There is no workholding limit: a chuck or collet has its own rated maximum rpm (often far below the spindle's), and the only place to state it today is the tool's `max_rpm`, which is the wrong owner (the same tool in a different chuck has a different limit).\n\n**Why it matters:** under G96 the rpm rises as the diameter shrinks, so a facing pass toward the centre runs up to the clamp. A clamp above the chuck's rating is a safety hazard (jaws lose grip force at speed).\n\n**Expected:** a setup on a lathe can state its workholding's maximum rpm. The G96 clamp (Fanuc `G50 S`, Tormach `G96 D`) and the G97 rpm check use min(machine, tool, workholding). An rpm above the workholding limit is refused by name, never silently capped. Undoable command plus MCP tool; old projects load with none.\n\n**Done means:** a setup with a 2000 rpm chuck limit posts `G50 S2000` / `G96 D2000` for a G96 op whose tool and machine allow more, and refuses a G97 op asking 2500 rpm by name; pinned in `lathe_post_css`; round-trip pinned.\n\nDepends on PR #990 merging (stack on it while it is open).\n",
